course: dictionary
lesson: 575
title: Authentication: proving it is really you
level: primary-6
stars: 1
explain: «Two-factor authentication asks for two things, your password and a code sent to your phone.» The clues are password and code. Both are ways of checking someone, so authentication must mean making sure a person really is who they say they are.
explain: The sentence gives the reason: «so a stolen password alone is not enough to get in.» If a thief steals your password, they still need the code on your phone. Two checks are safer than one.
explain: Authentication can also mean proving that a thing is real. A painting or a signature can be checked to see if it is genuine and not a fake. Ask yourself: is the sentence about checking a person, or checking whether something is real?
fact: Authentication means checking that someone or something really is what it says it is.
fact: «Two-factor authentication» means two checks, like a password plus a code sent to a phone.
fact: A fingerprint or a code can be used for authentication, just like a password.
fact: In art and history, authentication means proving that something is genuine and not a fake.
fact: Authentication is an uncountable noun, so we say «some authentication», never «an authentication».
exercise: What does «two-factor authentication» mean? | A phone that takes two photos at once | Sending two messages to two friends | *Two checks, like a password plus a code sent to a phone | Two passwords that are exactly the same
exercise: A website asks you for a password and then a code. What is it doing? | Sending a message to all your friends | Deleting your account for safety | Making the website look more colourful | *Checking that it really is you logging in
exercise: In art, what does authentication show about a painting? | That it is a fake and not worth looking at | That it was painted last week | *That it is genuine and not a fake | That it was painted with blue paint
test: Which of these is an example of authentication? | Choosing a new picture for your desktop | Printing a page from a website | Sharing a photo with your friends | *Typing a password to log in to a game
test: Why is «two-factor authentication» safer? | *Because a stolen password alone is not enough to get in | Because it is faster to type two checks | It is not safer than using one check | Because it stops every phone from working
exam: Which sentence uses authentication correctly? | *The bank asks for a code to check the customer is really who they say they are | The painting was authentication yesterday | Authentication is a type of fruit you can eat | I authenticated my lunch and then I ate it
